          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found points mainly to two dietary changes for high blood pressure.\n\n**Reducing sodium (salt).** The diet note (lifestyle-hypertension-diet) calls sodium reduction the most-studied dietary change for blood pressure. It says that cooking at home from unprocessed ingredients helps more than just removing the salt shaker, and that checking sodium per serving on food labels is more effective than any single swap. Most sodium usually comes from packaged and restaurant food. The same note says that combining sodium reduction with another dietary change works better than either alone.\n\n**Eating a pattern rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains and low-fat dairy, and low in saturated fat.** The cardiology guideline (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment) lists this as one of the lifestyle measures that every major body recommends, alongside regular activity, weight reduction where needed, limiting alcohol, and stopping smoking.\n\nThe search did not return specific amounts or named eating plans for blood pressure, and I have not added any from memory. A clinician or a registered dietitian can help work out what suits a particular person. Anyone who has been told they have high blood pressure should discuss it with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
